  "btech-cpc: recycle & bypass": {
    highlights: [
      "Recycle Operation in Chemical Processes: Returning a portion of a process output stream back to combine with fresh feed; Motivations: Recovering unreacted limiting reactants, improving overall conversion, enriching product concentrations, conserving expensive catalysts, and controlling reaction temperatures.",
      "Purge Stream Operation: Continuous bleeding of a small fraction of the recycle stream to prevent catastrophic steady-state accumulation of non-reactive inert impurities that enter with fresh feed.",
      "Bypass Operations: Diverting a fraction of the raw feed stream around a processing unit to blend directly with the product stream; Used for precise product composition control, temperature regulation, and dew point management.",
      "Definitions of Recycle Metrics: Recycle ratio $R_R = \\frac{\\text{Recycle flow rate}}{\\text{Fresh feed flow rate}}$ or $\\frac{\\text{Recycle}}{\\text{Gross feed}}$; Purge ratio $R_P = \\frac{\\text{Purge flow rate}}{\\text{Recycle flow rate}}$.",
      "Single-Pass vs Overall Conversion: Single-pass conversion across reactor unit: $f_{SP} = \\frac{\\text{Reactant reacted in reactor}}{\\text{Reactant entering reactor in gross feed}} = \\frac{n_{A,gross} - n_{A,reactor\\;out}}{n_{A,gross}}$; Overall conversion across entire plant: $f_{OV} = \\frac{\\text{Reactant reacted in plant}}{\\text{Reactant in fresh feed}} = \\frac{n_{A,fresh} - n_{A,plant\\;out}}{n_{A,fresh}}$.",
      "Mathematical Link Between Single-Pass and Overall Conversion: Overall conversion is substantially higher than single-pass conversion due to recycling of unreacted species ($f_{OV} > f_{SP}$); High overall conversions (~99%) achieved with modest single-pass conversions (~20-40%).",
      "Material Balance Protocol for Recycle Systems: 4 distinct balance envelopes: (1) Overall process envelope (around entire plant), (2) Mixing junction envelope (fresh feed + recycle = gross feed), (3) Reactor/process unit envelope, (4) Splitter / Separator envelope.",
      "Recycle with Chemical Reaction: Reversible reactions limited by chemical equilibrium (e.g. Haber-Bosch ammonia synthesis $\\text{N}_2 + 3\\text{H}_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2\\text{NH}_3$); Unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen separated by liquefaction and recycled back to synthesis loop.",
      "Purge Balance & Inert Steady-State Equilibrium: At steady-state, the rate of inert entering in the fresh feed must strictly equal the rate of inert exiting in the purge stream: $F z_{inert} = P y_{inert,purge}$; Dictates minimum required purge rate.",
      "Splitter Properties: Stream splitter divides a single stream into two or more streams; Compositions of all branching streams are identically equal: $x_{recycle} = x_{purge} = x_{separator\\;effluent}$.",
    ],
    keyPoints: [
      "Recycle Purpose: Maximizes overall conversion of expensive reactants and recycles heat/catalysts.",
      "Purge Purpose: Essential to prevent accumulation of inert materials in closed recycle loops.",
      "Single-Pass Conversion: $f_{SP} = \\frac{\\text{Reactant consumed in reactor}}{\\text{Reactant fed to reactor}}$.",
      "Overall Conversion: $f_{OV} = \\frac{\\text{Reactant consumed in plant}}{\\text{Reactant in fresh feed}}$.",
      "Conversion Comparison: Always $f_{OV} > f_{SP}$ in recycle systems.",
      "Splitter Rule: Splitting a stream alters flow rates but NEVER alters stream compositions ($x_1 = x_2 = x_3$).",
      "Mixing Point Balance: $\\text{Fresh Feed} + \\text{Recycle} = \\text{Gross Reactor Feed}$.",
      "Inert Balance Rule: $\\text{Inerts in Fresh Feed} = \\text{Inerts in Purge Stream}$ at steady state.",
      "Bypass Principle: Fluid diverted around unit without undergoing reaction/treatment.",
      "Exam Strategy: Start material balance by drawing the outer plant envelope to determine overall product yields before solving internal recycle loops.",
    ],
  },

  // =========================================================================
  // 8. BIOLOGY FOR ENGINEERS (QUALITATIVE - ZERO FORMULAS)
